## Solution

**Setup.** Let $y_i = x_i\sqrt{3}$. Then $y_i \in [-1, 3]$ and
$$\sum_{i=1}^{1997} y_i = \sqrt{3}\sum x_i = \sqrt{3}\cdot(-318\sqrt{3}) = -954.$$
Also $x_i^{12} = y_i^{12}/3^6 = y_i^{12}/729$, so maximizing $\sum x_i^{12}$ is equivalent to maximizing $\sum y_i^{12}$, then dividing by $729$.

**Convexity argument.** The function $f(y) = y^{12}$ is convex on $\mathbb{R}$ (since $f''(y) = 132\,y^{10} \ge 0$). The feasible set
$$P = \Big\{y \in [-1,3]^{1997} : \sum y_i = -954\Big\}$$
is a compact convex polytope. A convex function on a compact polytope attains its maximum at a vertex: if $v = \sum \lambda_j v_j$ is a convex combination of vertices, then $f(v) \le \sum \lambda_j f(v_j) \le \max_j f(v_j)$.

A vertex of $P$ has at least $1996$ of the $1997$ coordinates pinned at endpoints $\{-1, 3\}$ (one equality constraint plus $1996$ active box constraints). So at most one coordinate is interior.

**Finding the vertex.** Let $p$ coordinates equal $3$, $q$ coordinates equal $-1$, and (if needed) one coordinate equal $t \in [-1,3]$.

*Case 1: all at endpoints.* $p + q = 1997$ and $3p - q = -954$, giving $4p = 1043$, which has no integer solution. Infeasible.

*Case 2: one interior coordinate.* $p + q = 1996$ and $3p - q + t = -954$. Substituting $q = 1996 - p$:
$$4p + t = 1042 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad t = 1042 - 4p.$$
The constraint $t \in [-1, 3]$ gives $1039 \le 4p \le 1043$, i.e. $259.75 \le p \le 260.75$, so $p = 260$ is the unique integer solution. Then $t = 1042 - 1040 = 2 \in [-1,3]$ ✓, and $q = 1996 - 260 = 1736$.

Verification of the sum: $3(260) - 1736 + 2 = 780 - 1736 + 2 = -954$ ✓.

This is the unique vertex (up to permutation of coordinates), hence it gives the global maximum.

**Computing the value.**
$$\sum y_i^{12} = 260\cdot 3^{12} + 1736\cdot(-1)^{12} + 2^{12} = 260\cdot 531441 + 1736 + 4096.$$
$$260\cdot 531441 = 138{,}174{,}660, \qquad 1736 + 4096 = 5832.$$
$$\sum y_i^{12} = 138{,}174{,}660 + 5832 = 138{,}180{,}492.$$

Dividing by $729$:
$$\sum x_i^{12} = \frac{138{,}180{,}492}{729} = 189{,}548,$$
since $729 \times 189{,}548 = 137{,}781{,}000 + 399{,}492 = 138{,}180{,}492$ ✓.

**Extremizer.** The maximum is achieved by taking $260$ of the $x_i$ equal to $\sqrt{3}$, $1736$ of them equal to $-1/\sqrt{3}$, and one equal to $2/\sqrt{3}$.

$$\boxed{189548}$$

